     1	# ADR-0125: Claim Anatomy v2 — Temporal Scope, Sensitivity, Claim Type Registry
     2	
     3	**Status:** Accepted (substrate primitives for v1.4.0 spine; downstream enforcement in v1.4.1+)
     4	**Date:** 2026-04-24
     5	**Target:** v1.4.0 (schema + enums + registry); v1.4.1 (DOS-10 freshness + DOS-214 render policy); v1.4.2/3 (per-surface enforcement)
     6	**Extends:** [ADR-0113](0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md), [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md), [ADR-0114](0114-scoring-unification.md)
     7	**Pattern parallel:** [ADR-0115](0115-signal-granularity-audit.md) Signal Policy Registry
     8	
     9	## Context
    10	
    11	The 2026-04-24 Claim Anatomy Review (`.docs/plans/claim-anatomy-review-2026-04-24.md`) walked every dimension a claim should express in the v1.4.x substrate. Three dimensions were under-scrutinized at the substrate level and warrant a substrate primitive in v1.4.0 spine:
    12	
    13	1. **Temporal scope** (Review §11) — distinguishes PointInTime / State / Trend claims; affects freshness, supersession, and contradiction semantics.
    14	2. **Sensitivity** (Review §12) — claim-level sensitivity tier; structural safeguard against accidental surface leakage.
    15	3. **Claim type taxonomy** (Review §15) — currently free-form strings; should be a closed-but-extensible registry following the ADR-0115 Signal Policy Registry pattern.
    16	
    17	This ADR is the substrate allowance: schema fields + types + registry mechanism. Downstream enforcement (freshness scoring, render gates) is named per-dimension and lands in v1.4.1+.
    18	
    19	## Decision
    20	
    21	### 1. Temporal scope
    22	
    23	```rust
    24	pub enum TemporalScope {
    25	    /// Claim is about an event that happened at a moment in time.
    26	    /// Example: "Bob said X in 4/23 meeting." Doesn't go stale — the event occurred.
    27	    /// Freshness: never decays.
    28	    /// Supersession: cannot be superseded (different events are different facts).
    29	    /// Contradiction: usually doesn't contradict another PointInTime; may contradict a State.
    30	    PointInTime { occurred_at: DateTime<Utc> },
    31	
    32	    /// Claim is about a persistent condition until contradicted.
    33	    /// Example: "Bob is the champion." Has a half-life.
    34	    /// Freshness: decays per DOS-10 table.
    35	    /// Supersession: a newer State at same field_path supersedes.
    36	    /// Contradiction: State + State with different values is a contradiction.
    37	    State,
    38	
    39	    /// Claim is about evolution over time.
    40	    /// Example: "Engagement has been declining over the last 30 days."
    41	    /// Freshness: based on recency of latest underlying data point.
    42	    /// Supersession: recomputed; new trend on fresher data supersedes.
    43	    /// Contradiction: another Trend with opposite direction at same field_path.
    44	    Trend { window_start: DateTime<Utc>, window_end: DateTime<Utc> },
    45	}
    46	```
    47	
    48	Schema allowance:
    49	
    50	```sql
    51	ALTER TABLE intelligence_claims ADD COLUMN temporal_scope TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'state';
    52	```
    53	
    54	v1.4.0 spine ships the column + enum + serde impls. Default `State` for behavioral parity with current implicit assumption. v1.4.1 DOS-10 freshness factor consults `temporal_scope` to apply the right decay rule.
    55	
    56	### 2. Sensitivity
    57	
    58	```rust
    59	pub enum ClaimSensitivity {
    60	    /// Fine on customer-facing surfaces.
    61	    /// Example: public press release content; product feature mentions.
    62	    Public,
    63	
    64	    /// Fine on user's own surfaces, briefings, MCP. Never customer-facing or shared/published.
    65	    /// Example: CRM notes, internal Slack discussions.
    66	    /// **Default for claims sourced from internal systems.**
    67	    Internal,
    68	
    69	    /// Fine on the user's own surfaces only. Not on MCP, not on Publish, not on briefings shared with others.
    70	    /// Example: personal notes about a stakeholder's challenges.
    71	    Confidential,
    72	
    73	    /// Only the user themselves; not even trusted human analysts in multi-user (future).
    74	    /// Example: legal-confidential, HR-sensitive content.
    75	    UserOnly,
    76	}
    77	```
    78	
    79	Schema allowance:
    80	
    81	```sql
    82	ALTER TABLE intelligence_claims ADD COLUMN sensitivity TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'internal';
    83	```
    84	
    85	Default `Internal` is the conservative choice — most claims sourced from internal systems should default Internal. `Public` requires explicit author choice or source-class inheritance. `Confidential` / `UserOnly` require explicit author choice.
    86	
    87	v1.4.0 spine ships the column + enum. Render-time gates land per-surface in v1.4.1+ (see §4).
    88	
    89	### 3. Claim type registry
    90	
    91	Today `claim_type` is a free-form string. Every Transform ability defines its own implicitly. Dedup (per claim_type), freshness decay (per claim_type per DOS-10), commit policy gates, rendering policy — all key on this string. Drift is invisible.
    92	
    93	This ADR introduces `ClaimTypeRegistry` — a compile-time exhaustive const slice mapping every legal `claim_type` to its metadata:
    94	
    95	```rust
    96	pub struct ClaimTypeMetadata {
    97	    /// Canonical string written into the `intelligence_claims.claim_type` column.
    98	    pub name: &'static str,
    99	
   100	    /// Default temporal scope for this claim type. Authors may override per-claim
   101	    /// when the specific assertion warrants different semantics.
   102	    pub default_temporal_scope: TemporalScope,
   103	
   104	    /// Default sensitivity. Same author-override semantics.
   105	    pub default_sensitivity: ClaimSensitivity,
   106	
   107	    /// Freshness decay class — referenced by DOS-10's per-source / per-type half-life table.
   108	    pub freshness_decay_class: FreshnessDecayClass,
   109	
   110	    /// Commit policy class per ADR-0113 §3 — most types are Standard;
   111	    /// specific types may require gated commit (e.g. `tombstone` is always immediate).
   112	    pub commit_policy_class: CommitPolicyClass,
   113	
   114	    /// Which actor classes may write this claim type.
   115	    pub allowed_actor_classes: &'static [ClaimActorClass],
   116	
   117	    /// Which `SubjectRef` variants this claim type can attach to.
   118	    /// Example: `stakeholder_role` claims attach to `Person` only;
   119	    /// `account_health_band` attaches to `Account` only.
   120	    pub canonical_subject_types: &'static [SubjectType],
   121	}
   122	
   123	pub const CLAIM_TYPE_REGISTRY: &[ClaimTypeMetadata] = &[
   124	    ClaimTypeMetadata {
   125	        name: "stakeholder_role",
   126	        default_temporal_scope: TemporalScope::State,
   127	        default_sensitivity: ClaimSensitivity::Internal,
   128	        freshness_decay_class: FreshnessDecayClass::CrmField,
   129	        commit_policy_class: CommitPolicyClass::Standard,
   130	        allowed_actor_classes: &[ClaimActorClass::User, ClaimActorClass::Human, ClaimActorClass::Agent, ClaimActorClass::External],
   131	        canonical_subject_types: &[SubjectType::Person],
   132	    },
   133	    ClaimTypeMetadata {
   134	        name: "renewal_date",
   135	        default_temporal_scope: TemporalScope::State,
   136	        default_sensitivity: ClaimSensitivity::Internal,
   137	        freshness_decay_class: FreshnessDecayClass::SalesforceFieldUpdate,
   138	        commit_policy_class: CommitPolicyClass::Standard,
   139	        allowed_actor_classes: &[ClaimActorClass::User, ClaimActorClass::Agent, ClaimActorClass::External],
   140	        canonical_subject_types: &[SubjectType::Account],
   141	    },
   142	    // ... initial set covering DOS-218 (get_entity_context) + DOS-219 (prepare_meeting) outputs ~10-15 entries
   143	];
   144	```
   145	
   146	Enforcement (compile-time):
   147	
   148	- A claim row written with a `claim_type` not in the registry **fails CI lint**.
   149	- New claim types require an ADR amendment (or registry-extension PR with documented rationale linking back to this ADR).
   150	- The pattern parallels [ADR-0115](0115-signal-granularity-audit.md) Signal Policy Registry — same exhaustiveness check shape.
   151	
   152	v1.4.0 spine ships the registry mechanism + initial set covering the two pilot abilities (DOS-218 + DOS-219). New claim types are added incrementally as new abilities ship in v1.4.1+.
   153	
   154	### 4. Per-dimension downstream landing
   155	
   156	| Dimension | v1.4.0 spine | v1.4.1 | v1.4.2 | v1.4.3 |
   157	|---|---|---|---|---|
   158	| Temporal scope | column + enum + serde | DOS-10 freshness consults it; supersession semantics | — | — |
   159	| Sensitivity | column + enum + serde | DOS-214 render layer enforces | entity surfaces enforce ceiling | briefing surfaces enforce ceiling |
   160	| Claim type registry | mechanism + initial set + CI lint | extensions as new abilities ship | extensions | extensions |
   161	
   162	## Non-goals for spine
   163	
   164	- DOS-10 freshness formula consultation of `temporal_scope` (v1.4.1).
   165	- Per-surface sensitivity ceilings (v1.4.1+).
   166	- Render-time enforcement (v1.4.1+).
   167	- Counter-claim denormalized boolean (v1.4.1; deferred per anatomy review §13).
   168	- Causal lineage between claims (v1.5.x; deferred per anatomy review §14).
   169	- Locale on FieldAttribution (v1.4.1; deferred per anatomy review §17).
   170	- Decision relevance / actionability tier (v1.5.x; deferred per anatomy review §18).
   171	- Reversibility (lives at action / publish level; declared out of scope per anatomy review §16).
   172	
   173	## Consequences
   174	
   175	### Positive
   176	
   177	- Substrate has explicit fields for the three dimensions; downstream enforcement is additive and non-breaking.
   178	- **Temporal scope** makes "this event happened" structurally distinct from "this state holds" — eliminates the wrong-decay-on-PointInTime bug before it ships.
   179	- **Sensitivity** makes surface-leak prevention substrate-level instead of surface-policy convention. Surfaces that respect the sensitivity ceiling cannot leak Internal content into Public surfaces by construction.
   180	- **Claim type registry** forces deliberation on new claim types and prevents silent taxonomy drift. Adding a new claim type is now an explicit, reviewed event — not a string typed into a Transform ability.
   181	
   182	### Negative / risks
   183	
   184	- Three new schema fields. Acceptable; all default to safe values (`State`, `Internal`).
   185	- Registry maintenance burden — every new claim type requires a registration PR. Mitigated by mirroring ADR-0115 Signal Policy Registry pattern (proven on signals; same author cadence).
   186	- Authors may forget to set non-default `temporal_scope` and `sensitivity`. Mitigated by `ClaimTypeMetadata.default_*` values being looked up at write time when claim row leaves either field as default — the metadata supplies the right value for the type.
   187	
   188	### Neutral
   189	
   190	- v1.4.0 spine behavior is unchanged: defaults preserve current implicit assumptions until v1.4.1+ enforcement lands.
   191	- The registry pattern matches an existing precedent (ADR-0115); no new architectural concept.
   192	
   193	## References
   194	
   195	- [ADR-0113: Human and Agent Analysis as First-Class Claim Sources](0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md) — claim row schema; this ADR adds three fields.
   196	- [ADR-0105: Provenance as First-Class Output](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) — `Provenance.warnings` interacts with sensitivity at render-time masking.
   197	- [ADR-0114: Scoring Unification](0114-scoring-unification.md) — `freshness_weight` factor will consult `temporal_scope` in v1.4.1.
   198	- [ADR-0115: Signal Granularity, Policy Registry, and Durable Invalidation](0115-signal-granularity-audit.md) — pattern parallel for the claim type registry's compile-time exhaustiveness.
   199	- [ADR-0124: Longitudinal Topic Threading](0124-longitudinal-topic-threading.md) — same substrate-allowance pattern.
   200	- `.docs/plans/claim-anatomy-review-2026-04-24.md` — sourcing review (§11, §12, §15 for the three dimensions covered here).

