    90	Claims that do not pass the gate transition to `proposed` and become visible in the Analysis Inbox (v1.5.0 surface). They do not appear in briefings, MCP responses, or any default-read surface.
    91	
    92	### 4. Propose/commit mechanics
    93	
    94	Claim insertion is split across two ability categories:
    95	
    96	- **Transform abilities** produce `AbilityOutput<ClaimProposal>` — synthesized assertions with full provenance, but no side effect on `intelligence_claims`.
    97	- **Maintenance abilities** consume `ClaimProposal` + `AbilityContext` and apply the commit policy. The write itself happens inside a service function (`services/claims.rs::commit_claim` or `::propose_claim`) that honors [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) mode gates and [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) safety constraints.
    98	
    99	This matches [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §3's call-graph distinction — Transform abilities must not mutate. The propose/commit split is the structural enforcement of that boundary for claim production.
   100	
   101	Legacy path: where current enrichment writes claims directly (pre-v1.4.1), that write counts as a Maintenance ability call with a synthesized `ClaimProposal` and is subject to the same commit policy. The v1.4.1 enrichment refactor ([DOS-241](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-241)) is where this distinction becomes fully structural.
   102	
   103	### 5. Tombstones
   104	
   105	A tombstone is a claim with:
   106	
   107	- `actor = 'user'` (or `user_removal` for the specific tombstone variant when the user explicitly cleared a field)
   108	- `claim_text = NULL`
   109	- `retraction_reason = 'user_removal'`
   110	- `claim_state = 'committed'` (tombstones commit immediately per §3)
   111	
   112	Tombstones participate in supersede semantics: when a user removes a field value, a tombstone claim is written and supersedes any prior committed claim at the same `(entity_id, claim_type, field_path)`.
   113	
   114	**Writers must consult tombstones before proposing — PRE-GATE CHECK (clarified 2026-04-20 per outside voice finding #3).** The tombstone check is **not** part of the commit policy gate. It is a **hard pre-gate rejection** that runs inside `services::claims::propose_claim` before any commit policy evaluation. Order of operations:
   115	
   116	1. Tombstone check (this §5) — reject any actor targeting a tombstoned `field_path` within the tombstone window, regardless of commit policy config.
   117	2. Dedup check (§8) — match existing claim; increment corroboration if yes.
   118	3. Commit policy gate (§3) — if actor is `agent` and policy is `gated`, run trust + corroboration logic; otherwise `immediate`.
   119	
   120	This order matters because it means **the ghost-resurrection fix is live in v1.4.0 even when the commit policy config is `immediate` for all actor classes** (which R1.9 specifies for v1.4.0). The tombstone protection does not depend on the gate activating; it's a structural pre-check. When v1.4.1 flips agent policy to `gated`, the tombstone check still runs first.
   121	
   122	An agent wishing to override a tombstone must produce a higher-threshold corroboration (e.g., three independent sources within 7 days) and surface the override as a contradiction (§7) rather than a silent commit.
   123	
   124	This closes the ghost-resurrection bug documented in [ADR-0118](0118-dailyos-as-ai-harness-principles-and-residual-gaps.md) Gap D **in v1.4.0**, not deferred to v1.4.1.
   125	
   126	### 6. Agent trust ledger
   127	
   128	Each `agent:<name>:<version>` actor has a durable accuracy record:
   129	
   130	```sql
   131	CREATE TABLE agent_trust_ledger (
   132	  agent_name        TEXT NOT NULL,
   133	  agent_version     TEXT NOT NULL,
   134	  claim_type        TEXT NOT NULL,
   135	  alpha             REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 1.0,   -- corroborated / correct
   136	  beta              REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 1.0,   -- contradicted / incorrect
   137	  posterior_score   REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 0.5,   -- Beta posterior mean, cached
   138	  last_updated      TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,
   139	  PRIMARY KEY (agent_name, agent_version, claim_type)
   140	);
   141	```
   142	
   143	Updates:
   144	
   145	- User accepts an agent claim via corroboration or explicit confirmation → `alpha += 1`.

