     1	//! DOS-311: schema-epoch fence for `intelligence.json` writes.
     2	//!
     3	//! The fence prevents stale writes during the W3 / DOS-7 cutover. The flow:
     4	//!
     5	//! 1. A worker captures the current `migration_state.schema_epoch` at
     6	//!    job pickup via [`FenceCycle::capture`].
     7	//! 2. The worker does its enrichment / mutation work (potentially seconds
     8	//!    to minutes for PTY/Glean paths).
     9	//! 3. Before writing `intelligence.json`, the worker passes its `FenceCycle`
    10	//!    to [`fenced_write_intelligence_json`]. The fence re-reads the epoch;
    11	//!    if it has advanced (because DOS-7's migration ran mid-flight), the
    12	//!    write is rejected with [`FenceError::EpochAdvanced`].
    13	//! 4. The caller treats `EpochAdvanced` as a soft skip: log, do not roll
    14	//!    back DB state (DB is canonical), and re-enqueue the work for the
    15	//!    next cycle.
    16	//!
    17	//! ## Cross-issue dependency note
    18	//!
    19	//! The live DOS-311 ticket also requires `--repair` mode that consumes
    20	//! `services/claims.rs::commit_claim` (DOS-7) and a reconcile pass over
    21	//! `intelligence_claims` (DOS-7 schema). Both ship in W3. This module
    22	//! ships the substrate primitive (epoch capture + recheck on write); the
    23	//! reconcile + repair binary land alongside DOS-7.
